      "kind": "locked-English-neighbor",
      "source_text": "<STRUCT_0001>\n<LOCKED_0001>\nLet <MATH_0001> be a ring. Let <MATH_0002> be a cosimplicial <MATH_0003>-algebra.\nIn this setting a descent datum corresponds to an cosimplicial\n<MATH_0004>-module <MATH_0005> with the property that for\nevery <MATH_0006> and every <MATH_0007> the\nmap <MATH_0008> induces an isomorphism\n<MATH_0009>\nLet us call such a cosimplicial module a <TEXTITOPEN_0001>cartesian module<TEXTITCLOSE_0001>.\nIn this setting, the proof of Proposition <REF_0001>\ncan be split in the following steps\n<STRUCT_0002>\n<STRUCT_0003> If <MATH_0010> and <MATH_0011> are faithfully flat,\nthen descent data for <MATH_0012> are effective if\ndescent data for <MATH_0013> are effective.\n<STRUCT_0004> Let <MATH_0014> be an <MATH_0015>-algebra. Descent data for <MATH_0016> correspond\nto cartesian <MATH_0017>-modules.\n<STRUCT_0005> If <MATH_0018> has a section then <MATH_0019> is homotopy\nequivalent to <MATH_0020>, the constant cosimplicial\n<MATH_0021>-algebra with value <MATH_0022>.\n<STRUCT_0006> If <MATH_0023> is a homotopy equivalence of\ncosimplicial <MATH_0024>-algebras then the functor\n<MATH_0025>\ninduces an equivalence of categories between cartesian\n<MATH_0026>-modules and cartesian <MATH_0027>-modules.\n<STRUCT_0007>\nFor (1) see Lemma <REF_0002>.\nPart (2) uses Lemma <REF_0003>.\nPart (3) we have seen in the proof of Lemma <REF_0004>\n(it relies on Simplicial,\nLemma <REF_0005>).\nMoreover, part (4) is a triviality if you think about it right!\n<STRUCT_0008>",
